\title{Perfect Simulation, Random-Point Soundness, and Local Verification\\
\large A Unified Mathematical Account of Zero-Knowledge Verifiable Computation}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{29 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We present a self-contained mathematical framework connecting perfect zero-knowledge simulation, the graph three-coloring protocol, polynomial random-point verification, and a constant-query bridge to probabilistically checkable proofs. Perfect interactive zero knowledge is exact equality between the distribution of a verifier's real view and a simulator distribution depending only on the public statement. Its non-interactive analogue additionally requires every proof in the support of the honest distribution to be accepted. For graph three-colorability, random permutation of color labels preserves properness and makes the transcript on a challenged properly colored edge exactly uniform over ordered pairs of distinct colors. For a simplified quadratic-arithmetic-program verifier over a finite field, a false identity $p=ht$ passes a random-point test at no more than $\deg(p-ht)$ points; conversely, passing at more points forces the identity. Finally, a graph-coloring oracle verifier reads at most two symbols per edge, and every alleged coloring of a non-three-colorable graph is rejected on at least one query. These results isolate a common architecture: small local observations, structural consistency bounds, and simulation-based privacy.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Verifiable computation asks how a resource-limited verifier can gain confidence in a claim without reproducing all of the claimant's work. Zero-knowledge proof adds privacy: verification should reveal nothing about a secret witness beyond what follows from the public claim. Succinct arguments and probabilistically checkable proofs approach the same economy by compressing global correctness into a few algebraic checks or local oracle queries.

We develop a bridge among these ideas. We first give distributional definitions of perfect interactive and non-interactive zero knowledge. We then study graph three-coloring, where a witness is hidden by a random permutation of color names. Next we analyze a simplified polynomial verifier: a claimed identity is tested at a random field point, and root counting bounds false acceptance. Finally, graph coloring is represented as a locally testable oracle in which each edge check reads only two symbols.

The scope is precise. The graph theorem gives perfect zero knowledge against the prescribed honest verifier. The polynomial theorem proves the soundness of a random-point identity check, not every cryptographic property of a deployed succinct argument. The local graph verifier establishes constant query complexity and existence of a rejecting query on false instances, not a graph-size-independent rejection gap.

\section{Distributional Proof Systems}

\subsection{Probability distributions}

Let $X$ be a finite set. A probability mass function is a map $\mu:X\to[0,1]$ satisfying $\sum_{x\in X}\mu(x)=1$. Two distributions are equal exactly when their masses agree at every point. The support is
\[
\supp(\mu)=\{x\in X:\mu(x)>0\}.
\]

\begin{definition}[Perfect interactive zero knowledge]
Let $S$ be a set of public statements and $V$ a space of verifier views. For each $s\in S$, let $R_s$ be the real-view distribution and $M_s$ a simulator distribution computed from $s$ alone. The system is perfectly zero knowledge on valid statements if $R_s=M_s$ whenever $s$ is valid.
\end{definition}

The view includes all verifier observations: random coins, messages, challenges, responses, and public derived data. The simulator receives no witness.

\begin{theorem}[Pointwise equality of interactive views]
For every valid statement $s$ and every view $v$,
\[
R_s(v)=M_s(v).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Equality of distributions is equality of their mass functions. Evaluate the equal functions at $v$.
\end{proof}

Consequently every event $A\subseteq V$ has equal probability in the real and simulated experiments, since summing pointwise equal masses gives $\Prb_{R_s}[A]=\Prb_{M_s}[A]$.

\begin{definition}[Perfect non-interactive zero knowledge]
Let $P$ be a finite proof space and $\operatorname{Verify}(s,\pi)$ the acceptance predicate. Let $H_s$ be the honest proof distribution and $M_s$ a simulator distribution depending only on $s$. The system is perfectly non-interactive zero knowledge if, for each valid $s$, every $\pi\in\supp(H_s)$ is accepted and $H_s=M_s$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Pointwise simulation and support acceptance]
For every valid $s$ and $\pi\in P$, $H_s(\pi)=M_s(\pi)$. If $H_s(\pi)>0$, then $\operatorname{Verify}(s,\pi)$ is true.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The equality follows by evaluating $H_s=M_s$ at $\pi$. The second statement is the support-acceptance condition.
\end{proof}

Privacy and acceptance are separate obligations: simulation controls disclosure, while support correctness prevents honest generation of rejected proofs.

\section{Graph Three-Coloring}

Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite graph and $C=\{0,1,2\}$. A coloring is a function $c:V\to C$. It is proper if $c(u)\ne c(v)$ for every $(u,v)\in E$.

In one protocol round, the prover chooses a uniform permutation $\pi$ of $C$, commits to $\pi(c(v))$ for each vertex $v$, and opens the two commitments on an edge selected by the verifier. The verifier accepts when the openings are valid and the endpoint colors differ. The theorem below concerns the permuted-color transcript; hiding and binding of commitments form a separate cryptographic layer.

\begin{lemma}[Permutation preserves inequality]
If $a,b\in C$, $a\ne b$, and $\pi$ is a permutation of $C$, then $\pi(a)\ne\pi(b)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
A permutation is injective. Equality of the images would imply $a=b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Perfect completeness]
If $c$ is a proper three-coloring, then $v\mapsto\pi(c(v))$ is proper for every permutation $\pi$ of $C$. Thus every challenged edge is accepted.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For each $(u,v)\in E$, properness gives $c(u)\ne c(v)$; the lemma gives $\pi(c(u))\ne\pi(c(v))$.
\end{proof}

Fix $a\ne b$ and define
\[
D=\{(x,y)\in C^2:x\ne y\}.
\]
The real opened-color transcript is $(\pi(a),\pi(b))$ for uniform $\pi$. The simulator samples uniformly from $D$.

\begin{lemma}[Permutation--pair bijection]
For fixed $a\ne b$, the map
\[
\Phi_{a,b}:\operatorname{Sym}(C)\to D,
\qquad \Phi_{a,b}(\pi)=(\pi(a),\pi(b))
\]
is a bijection.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The image lies in $D$ by injectivity. Given $(x,y)\in D$, exactly one permutation sends $a$ to $x$ and $b$ to $y$: the remaining source color must map to the remaining target color.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Perfect honest-verifier zero knowledge]
For every pair of distinct endpoint colors $a,b$, the real transcript distribution of $(\pi(a),\pi(b))$ is exactly the witness-independent uniform distribution on $D$. In particular,
\[
\Prb[(\pi(a),\pi(b))=(x,y)] =
\begin{cases}
1/6,&x\ne y,\\
0,&x=y.
\end{cases}
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The preceding bijection maps six equiprobable permutations to the six elements of $D$, so each distinct ordered pair has probability $1/6$.
\end{proof}

If an alleged coloring is improper, some edge is monochromatic. Permuting labels preserves equality, so this defect cannot be hidden if that edge is challenged. Uniform edge sampling detects at least one bad edge with probability at least $1/|E|$; repetition amplifies detection. The exact simulation theorem is honest-verifier zero knowledge because the challenge follows the prescribed experiment. Arbitrary malicious strategies generally require rewinding or another simulator construction.

\section{Random-Point Verification of Polynomial Identities}

Let $F$ be a finite field. Given $p,h,t\in F[x]$, the intended identity is $p=ht$. The verifier samples $s\in F$ and accepts if $p(s)=h(s)t(s)$. Define the discrepancy
\[
q=p-ht.
\]
Passing points are exactly roots of $q$.

\begin{lemma}[Root bound]
A nonzero polynomial $q\in F[x]$ has at most $\deg q$ distinct roots in $F$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induct on degree. A nonzero constant has no roots. If $q(a)=0$, the factor theorem gives $q=(x-a)r$ with $\deg r=\deg q-1$. All roots other than $a$ are roots of $r$, and induction completes the bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Random-point soundness]
If $p\ne ht$, then
\[
\left|\{s\in F:p(s)=h(s)t(s)\}\right|\le \deg(p-ht).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The discrepancy $q=p-ht$ is nonzero, and the passing points are its roots. Apply the root bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Uniform false-acceptance probability]
For a uniform $s\in F$ and $p\ne ht$,
\[
\Prb[p(s)=h(s)t(s)]\le \min\left(1,\frac{\deg(p-ht)}{|F|}\right).
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Too many passing points force identity]
If
\[
\deg(p-ht)<\left|\{s\in F:p(s)=h(s)t(s)\}\right|,
\]
then $p=ht$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $p\ne ht$, random-point soundness contradicts the strict inequality. Hence the discrepancy is zero.
\end{proof}

For a sharp example, work over $F_{101}$ and let
\[
t(x)=x^2+1,\qquad h(x)=3x+2,
\]
\[
p(x)=h(x)t(x)+x(x-1)(x-2).
\]
Then $q(x)=x(x-1)(x-2)$ has degree $3$ and roots exactly $0,1,2$. The false identity passes at $3$ of $101$ points. This model isolates the soundness engine; witness hiding, commitments, setup assumptions, and extraction require additional constructions.

\section{A Two-Query PCP Bridge}

Encode an alleged coloring as an oracle $\Pi:V\to C$. For an edge $e=(u,v)$, query the set $Q(e)=\{u,v\}$ and accept exactly when $\Pi(u)\ne\Pi(v)$.

\begin{theorem}[Two-query locality]
For every edge $e$, $|Q(e)|\le 2$, independently of graph size.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The set is generated by the two endpoints, so it contains at most two elements.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Existence of a rejecting local query]
If $G$ is not three-colorable, then for every oracle $\Pi:V\to C$ there exists $(u,v)\in E$ with $\Pi(u)=\Pi(v)$. The two-query verifier rejects on that edge.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If every edge had differently colored endpoints, $\Pi$ would itself be a proper three-coloring, contradicting the hypothesis.
\end{proof}

This is the deterministic soundness core of the PCP connection. It guarantees one rejecting edge, not a constant fraction. Uniform edge sampling therefore has only the basic lower bound $1/|E|$ without gap amplification. A full constant-gap construction requires a richer encoding that makes every false proof far from acceptance.

\section{Algorithms and Complexity}

\paragraph{Transcript enumeration.}
Enumerate all six color permutations and count $(\pi(a),\pi(b))$. Explicit generation over $k$ colors costs $O(k!\,k)$ time and $O(k^2)$ output storage. For $k=3$, every distinct ordered pair receives mass $1/6$.

\paragraph{Finite-field audit.}
Evaluate $p,h,t$ at every point of a prime field using Horner's method. If the field has $r$ elements and the relevant degree is $d$, exhaustive auditing costs $O(rd)$ field operations. A one-challenge verifier uses $O(d)$ operations and constant auxiliary space.

\paragraph{Local graph audit.}
Scan edges until a monochromatic one is found. The worst-case running time is $O(|E|)$ with $O(1)$ auxiliary storage; each test reads at most two oracle symbols.

\section{Applications and Discussion}

Random relabeling supports confidential constraint satisfaction: it preserves the relation being checked while hiding semantic labels. Polynomial evaluation supports outsourced computation: arithmetized inconsistency becomes a nonzero discrepancy whose degree bounds successful deception. Local oracle tests separate proof length from query count and can be combined with hiding commitments so that unopened coordinates remain private.

The three mechanisms share a pattern. A large witness is compressed into a small observation. A rigidity theorem protects the compression: permutation injectivity, polynomial root bounds, or the existence of a bad edge. Repetition or amplification strengthens local evidence. Simulation or hiding limits disclosure.

The distinctions are equally important. Honest-verifier simulation does not automatically handle malicious verifier strategies. A polynomial identity test alone does not provide zero knowledge or a cryptographic extractor. Existence of one rejecting edge does not give a constant soundness gap. Support correctness in the non-interactive definition is also indispensable: privacy of a proof distribution does not imply that its support is accepted.

\section{Future Work}

Natural extensions include a polynomial-time rewinding simulator for malicious graph-protocol verifiers; a random-oracle analysis of hash-derived polynomial challenges; polynomial blinding by random multiples of the target polynomial; gap amplification yielding graph-size-independent rejection probability; and composition of committed local oracles with perfect hiding and simulation of opened coordinates.

\section{Conclusion}

Perfect simulation, random-point soundness, and local verification are facets of a common methodology. A verifier sees only a small projection of a large witness. Invalid objects cannot make every projection consistent: a bad coloring has a bad edge, and a false polynomial identity has only boundedly many passing points. Privacy holds when the permitted projection can be reproduced from public information alone. For graph coloring, uniform relabeling yields exact witness-independent transcripts and perfect completeness. For simplified polynomial verification, discrepancy root counting gives a degree-over-field-size soundness bound. For local graph verification, every test reads at most two symbols and every false instance forces a rejecting query.

