The three mechanisms compress a large witness into a small view. Symmetry makes the graph view simulatable; algebraic rigidity bounds deceptive evaluations; locality turns a global graph defect into a two-symbol rejection. Together they explain how verification can become economical without indiscriminate disclosure.

